      "subsections": {
        "OVERVIEW": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Congenital ureteral orifice(s) is inapprop riately positioned caudal to the bladder trigone (i.e., trigone, urethra, vagina, vestibule, uterus, or prostate), resulting in incontinence.",
              "pdf_page": 516,
              "printed_page": 434,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "A common cause of urinary incontinence in juvenile female dogs. Also seen in adult dogs.",
              "pdf_page": 516,
              "printed_page": 434,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Dogs—>95% tunnel intramurally, traversing the urethra in the submucosa.",
              "pdf_page": 516,
              "printed_page": 434,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Male dogs—commonly associated with severe hydronephrosis and hydroureter due to ureteral opening stenosis.",
              "pdf_page": 516,
              "printed_page": 434,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Commonly associated with multiple anomalies of the urinary tract—including concurrent urethral sphincter mechanism incompetence (USMI), hydroureter, hydronephrosis, short urethra/intrapelvic bladder.",
              "pdf_page": 516,
              "printed_page": 434,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
